# A multi-pattern Species observable over complexes that merge and split
# (issue #81).
#
# Where multi_pattern_species_obs_model pins the cost of the defect, this
# one pins the semantics and the bookkeeping the fix rests on.
#
# `Mixed` and `Chains` each carry two patterns that can match the SAME
# complex, and the full walk counts such a complex once per pattern:
#
#   Mixed   A()>=3, B()==1   different quantity relations, different
#                            seed types — a chain of three or more A's
#                            carrying exactly one B satisfies both.
#   Chains  A(s!+), A(s!+,t!+)
#                            same seed type, one pattern a strict subset
#                            of the other — a chain with an interior A
#                            satisfies both.
#
# So the per-complex tally cannot be shared across an observable's
# patterns: each pattern needs its own count and its own verdict.  The
# seeded `A(s!1,t,b!3).A(s!2,t!1,b).A(s,t!2,b).B(a!3)` trimer makes that
# checkable by hand at t=0, before any event — every copy contributes 2
# to `Mixed` and 2 to `Chains`, where one merged tally per complex would
# contribute 1.
#
# The rate law reads `Mixed` and `Chains`, so both take the per-event
# flush.  Each has a twin declaring the same patterns and read by
# nothing, and the two twins are unread in different ways:
#
#   MixedWalk   patterns are structurally unconstrained (`A()`, `B()`),
#               so an unread observable of this shape is not tracked at
#               all — the engine full-walks it at every sample.  That
#               makes it a from-scratch oracle for `Mixed` on every
#               sample row rather than only at t_end.
#   ChainsCtl   patterns carry bond constraints, so it IS tracked, and
#               keeps the ordinary once-per-sample flush cadence.  It
#               shares the per-molecule contribution tables and the
#               pool's dead-complex side channel with `Chains` — and
#               that channel is drained, not copied, so a per-event
#               flush that swallowed a notification the per-sample twin
#               still needed shows up here as the two disagreeing.
#
# A(s,t) polymerises into chains rather than capping at dimers, so a bond
# op moves molecules that are not themselves bond endpoints between
# complexes — the case per-complex tallies are most exposed to.  The rate
# law drives P -> Q, a pool the polymerisation chemistry never touches,
# so the observables move for reasons entirely outside the rule whose
# propensity reads them.
#
# Paired with: tests/cpp/multi_pattern_species_obs_test.cpp

begin model
begin parameters
    A_tot   120
    B_tot   60
    T_tot   12
    P_tot   400
    kf      0.02
    kr      0.05
    kfb     0.03
    krb     0.08
    kt      0.001
end parameters

begin molecule types
    A(s,t,b)
    B(a)
    P()
    Q()
end molecule types

begin seed species
    A(s,t,b)                                     A_tot
    B(a)                                         B_tot
    A(s!1,t,b!3).A(s!2,t!1,b).A(s,t!2,b).B(a!3)  T_tot
    P()                                          P_tot
    Q()                                          0
end seed species

begin observables
    Species    Mixed      A()>=3, B()==1
    Species    MixedWalk  A()>=3, B()==1
    Species    Chains     A(s!+), A(s!+,t!+)
    Species    ChainsCtl  A(s!+), A(s!+,t!+)
    Molecules  A_bound    A(s!+)
    Molecules  Q_tot      Q()
end observables

begin functions
    mixed_rate() = kt*(Mixed + Chains)
end functions

begin reaction rules
    A(s) + A(t) <-> A(s!1).A(t!1)   kf, kr
    A(b) + B(a) <-> A(b!1).B(a!1)   kfb, krb
    P() -> Q()                      mixed_rate()
end reaction rules
end model

begin actions
simulate({method=>"nf",t_end=>60,n_steps=>12,print_functions=>1})
end actions
